%I A401702 #10 Nov 16 2026 12:48:59
%S A401702 2,13,17,29,109,113,127,151,173,211,223,227,239,241,251,277,283,317,
%T A401702 347,419,463,467,557,569,587,631,643,661,821,857,887,907,1009,1033,
%U A401702 1087,1097,1163,1213,1237,1307,1433,1459,1499,1523,1543,1567,1657,1693,1709,1723,1759,1931,1999,2053,2081,2143,2153,2251,2281
%N A401702 Primes p for which there exists a noncomposite q <= p such that 2*(p# / q#) == -1 mod r, where p# = A034386(p), and r is the least prime larger than p.
%C A401702 See comments in A400843.
%H A401702 Antti Karttunen, <a href="/A401702/b401702.txt">Table of n, a(n) for n = 1..4102</a>
%H A401702 <a href="/index/Pri#primorial_numbers">Index entries for sequences related to primorial numbers</a>.
%F A401702 a(n) = A000040(A401059(n)).
%F A401702 a(n) = A064989(A400843(n)).
%e A401702 2 is a term because 1 + 2*(2# / q#) will be a multiple of 3 (next prime larger than 2) when we set also q to 2.
%e A401702 13 is a term because 1 + 2*(13# / q#) will be a multiple of 17 (next prime larger than 13) when we set q to 1, as then 1+(2*2*3*5*7*11*13) = 60061 = 17*3533.
%e A401702 17 is a term because 1 + 2*(17# / q#) will be a multiple of 19 (next prime larger than 17) when we set q to 2, as then 1+(2*3*5*7*11*13*17) = 510511 = 19*97*277.
%o A401702 (PARI)
%o A401702 A034386(n) = prod(i=1, primepi(n), prime(i));
%o A401702 is_A401702(p) = if(!isprime(p), 0, my(prp=A034386(p), r=nextprime(1+p)); if((r-1)==((2*prp)%r), 1, forprime(q=2,p,if((r-1)==(((2*prp)/A034386(q))%r), return(1))); (0))); \\ (reference implementation, very inefficient)
%Y A401702 Cf. A000040, A002110, A034386, A064989, A400843, A401059.
%Y A401702 Cf. also A401700, A401701, A401703.
%K A401702 nonn,new
%O A401702 1,1
%A A401702 _Antti Karttunen_, Nov 16 2026
